CORE no-new-smt
main.c

^\[main\.assertion\.29\] line \d+ reachable: FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of 29 failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
bits, .*: FAILURE$
--
Converting an integer to floating-point must be exact when the integer has at
most as many bits as the significand. For widths 3, 5, 9, 17 and 33 (one more
than a power of two), the bit-vector encodings of the conversion used an
exponent too narrow to hold the width minus one, and a normalisation shifter
that could not shift by the width minus one, so that even 1 and -1 converted
to wrong values. The last assertion
checks that the assumptions are satisfiable.
